Answer: Provide constructors with different parameter lists. The following program supports object creation with no details, only an ID, an ID and name, or all three values. The this keyword assigns each parameter to the corresponding instance variable.

```java
class User {    int id;    String name;    String email;    User() {        // No-argument constructor    }    User(int id) {        this.id = id;    }    User(int id, String name) {        this.id = id;        this.name = name;    }    User(int id, String name, String email) {        this.id = id;        this.name = name;        this.email = email;    }    void register() {        System.out.println(name + " is registered");    }    void login() {        System.out.println(name + " has logged in");    }}
public class UserApplication {    public static void main(String[] args) {        User user1 = new User();        User user2 = new User(10);        User user3 = new User(20, "Asha");        User user4 = new User(30, "Raghu", "raghu@example.com");        System.out.println(user1.id + " " + user1.name + " " + user1.email);        System.out.println(user2.id + " " + user2.name + " " + user2.email);        System.out.println(user3.id + " " + user3.name + " " + user3.email);        System.out.println(user4.id + " " + user4.name + " " + user4.email);        user4.register();        user4.login();    }}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output:0 null null10 null null20 Asha null30 Raghu raghu@example.comRaghu is registeredRaghu has logged in
